{"format":1,"seq":107,"writes":[{"type":"entry","value":{"model":[{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm thinking the collapse logic should run inside the APPLY_STAGED_EDITS task's commit so it has access to the full active view — the hook would always schedule this task when collapse is enabled or pending, and the task itself would read the active range, compute the view and run boundaries, select collapse targets, and write a single entry combining staged and collapse edits. Now I need to figure out how to detect run boundaries based on user activity.\n\nIdentifying runs is tricky since conversations mix user input entries, custom host messages, and pi-action comments — I'm weighing whether a run should be delimited purely by input entries or whether steering/follow-up messages mid-run complicate that. One option is passing the current run's `first` to the task and recording run boundaries in entry data each time an entry is written after a run, but that breaks down for older runs where collapse wasn't enabled or no edit was ever written, so I'm leaning toward deriving boundaries directly from existing entries rather than relying on a separate record.\n\nSince items with id >= run.first are already protected, splitting older runs on steering messages only makes collapse counting slightly more aggressive, which seems acceptable. I wonder if pi-durable records explicit per-run markers, so I should check the entry kinds more directly rather than inferring boundaries from inputs — grouping older items into runs based on id ranges below run.first seems like the simplest path forward.\n\nThinking about keepRuns semantics: the current (just-finished) run itself counts as one of the kept runs, since a follow-up naturally refers back to the run right before it. So with keepRuns=1, the current run stays verbatim and everything before it collapses — confirmed by the three-run example where after the third run, the first two collapse but the third remains untouched.\n\nWith keepRuns=0 this would collapse the current run itself, which must never happen, so I should enforce keepRuns as an integer >= 1. I'm also working out that minTokens should gate collapsing based on the active view's token count, likely defaulting to something like 32K so short threads with few runs aren't touched.\n\nBut I realize the system prompt alone can be 41K tokens, which would make a 32K threshold over the total context always exceeded trivially — so it makes more sense to compare only against the conversation's own tokens, excluding the system prompt, tools, and other fixed sections.\n\nI'll settle on a default minTokens around 32K measured against transcript tokens, reasoning that below that size the collapse doesn't save much and verbatim history helps short threads. For tests, I'll explicitly set minTokens to 0 so the small faux contexts still trigger collapsing, while leaving the 32K default safe for existing durable tests with small real contexts.\n\nFor which entries to collapse, I'm thinking through the criteria: assistant items with tool calls, in original state, unprotected, not pending, not already targeted by this run's staged edits, and whose results are similarly untouched -- skipping entirely if any result was elided. The size check compares the item plus its results' token cost against the token cost of the replacement note.\n\nI also need to restructure so `buildContextView` and an active-range reader work within a single transaction, letting the task read staged entries and build the view together -- conveniently, entries covered by the model's staged edits show up as pending in that view, so the collapse logic skips them automatically without extra handling.\n\nFor idempotence: since the task commits atomically, a rerun after crash only happens before creation, and once collapsed entries have state \"replaced\" or \"collapsed\" they're no longer \"original,\" so a later rerun won't re-collapse them. I should add a test that runs a fourth pass after run three's collapse and confirms nothing gets double-collapsed.\n\nI also need to add ContextState \"collapsed\" and ContextOperation \"collapse\" to the outline schema, checking how session.ts and the shared type declarations consume these enums — and verify forks correctly inherit the collapsed view from their fork point without being affected by later collapses in the parent.\n\nFor the edit entry's provenance, I'll use one aggregated collapse operation covering all collapsed assistant ids and results, with total tokens freed, rather than emitting a separate operation per call.\n\nNow I'm thinking about the hook: currently it skips scheduling when there's nothing pending, but with collapse enabled it needs to run whenever candidates might exist. Rather than hardcoding, I should turn the hook into a factory that takes the collapse config so it can decide to schedule based on pending edits or collapse eligibility, passing collapse options through to the task input.\n\nI'm also deciding whether the token-threshold check belongs in the hook or the task, and settling on doing it in the task since the hook can't cheaply build the needed view. I'm sketching the task input shape with optional collapse fields for backward compatibility with older in-flight tasks, and drafting the format for the collapsed-call note line that gets logged.\n\nWhen an assistant entry has multiple parallel tool calls, I need a note format that joins per-call descriptions together rather than just handling the single-call case.\n\nFor bounding the note to 512 bytes, I'm deciding a priority order for what gets trimmed first: functions list, then label, then error text, while entry IDs and token sizes are never cut since they're needed to recover originals.\n\nWith multiple calls, I'll apply progressive truncation: try the full middle string, then drop the function list, then cut each error line to a bounded length, and finally hard-cut the whole middle to fit the byte budget with an ellipsis. Labels stay capped at 160 characters and errors at 120, while token size and inspectEntry IDs are never truncated.\n\nI need to list both the call ID and every result ID, not just one. I'll settle on listing them explicitly when there are few, like `originals: session.inspectEntry(\"212\"), session.inspectEntry(\"213\")`, and switch to the compact \"for\" form when there are more IDs to cover.\n\nFor byte measurement I'll use TextEncoder rather than Node's Buffer, to keep things host-safe. For labeling, I'm thinking through a resolution order: use a presentation title if available, else the first code line, else the function ID, else a generic fallback — and I need to design a helper that produces both a summary of which functions ran and the outcome (success or error) for display purposes.\n\nI notice present's separator differs from the one used in the issue example, so I should refactor to export functions as an array rather than a pre-joined string, letting callers choose their own separator. I'm sketching a `digestTypeScriptCall` export with a title, functions list, and outcome string that captures ok/failed/asked/saved states consistently.\n\nFor non-TypeScript calls, the outcome just reduces to the tool name plus ok/failed status. I'm also realizing I need to check context-planning.test.ts to see how synthetic views are constructed, since that's likely where I should unit test the line-building logic directly rather than through the full harness.\n\nActually, tracing the sequence more carefully: consecutive assistant messages after collapse get merged by Anthropic's API automatically, so I need to check whether this test runner has credentials or routes through a provider that behaves the same way.\n\nSeparately, I'm reconsidering the collapse behavior for thinking blocks—the assistant entry preserves its text and thinking with signatures, and its tool calls plus results get flattened into a plain-text note appended to the content. But Anthropic only honors thinking blocks in the final assistant turn when tool use follows; earlier ones get stripped by the API regardless.\n\nI should check how pi-ai's Anthropic provider handles consecutive assistant messages and token accounting for kept entries, plus look into the context-planning tests and session.ts usages of state/operation.\n\n","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_bdrk_01QpXBkP7KFrVjJgzMNn9ZMc","name":"typescript","arguments":{"code":"// @pit: {\"label\": \"Probe env provider keys, session.ts state usage, planning test head\"}\nasync ({ shell: { exec }, workspace: { read, search } }) => {\n  const env = await exec(`env | cut -d= -f1 | grep -iE 'key|token|anthropic|aws|bedrock|nvidia|openai|model|pi_' | sort`);\n  const s = await search(\"elided|summarized|ContextState|ContextOperation|operation\", { path: \"src\", regex: true, limit: 80, contextLines: 0 });\n  const p = await read(\"test/durable/context-planning.test.ts\", { format: \"raw\", limit: 120 });\n  return { env: env.stdout, s: s.matches?.map((m: any) => `${m.file}:${m.line}: ${m.text}`) ?? s, p: p.content };\n}"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"nvidia","model":"aws/anthropic/bedrock-claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":7717,"cacheRead":62966,"cacheWrite":2797,"totalTokens":73482,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0,"totalFromProvider":false},"cacheWrite1h":0,"reasoning":7392},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1794904464345,"responseId":"msg_bdrk_ap2aqtsalpnevrnxc65qqjzsnq57m2nzqc5av3o6afhbq3oxrw7a","rawStopReason":"tool_use","durationMs":82668,"thinkingLevel":"medium"}],"kind":"pi.assistant","id":35,"conversationId":1,"byTaskId":34}},{"type":"task","value":{"id":34,"conversationId":1,"kind":"pi.generation","version":2,"input":{},"background":false,"abortRequested":false,"state":{"status":"waiting","checkpoint":{"phase":"tools","assistant":35,"tools":[36],"pending":[]},"on":[36],"policy":"allSettled"},"sta